WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.060
If you control who gets to practice a profession

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:04.490
you also get to control how it's practiced.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.950
The licensure effort, that's an attempt to
silence CrossFit on the subject of nutrition

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:09.950
and exercise.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:15.720
The state legislature that would adopt a state-wide
licensure program for exercise

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:20.980
is composed of people who do not understand
anything about the squat.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.660
The federal government has designed this food
pyramid.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:26.960
For the past three decades,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.320
almost everything the federal government has
told the public about eating a healthy diet

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.660
may have been wrong.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:37.340
The nutrition guidelines that were set in
the late 1970s

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.360
that was primarily politics.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.880
When it comes to calories, fat is the enemy.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:46.210
The surgeon general told Americans to avoid
saturated fats

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:49.020
and prioritize grains and other carbohydrates.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.500
Low-fat products began filling the aisles
at grocery stores

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.240
as families tried to follow the government’s
famous food pyramid.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:01.080
At it's base are foods like breads and pastas
that should be eaten in greatest quantity.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.860
As obesity continued to climb in America,

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:08.120
some dissenting scientists and experts began
to question the consensus,

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:12.160
but the US government and major health organizations
were slow to react,

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.620
only now beginning to update their recommendations.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:20.020
And some fitness professionals are afraid
that what happened to the field of nutrition

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.640
is about to happen to exercise.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.260
All of these government agencies, all of our
universities,

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:31.860
they've all sat silent through one of the
worst declines in health

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.540
the modern world's ever seen.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.940
And they're response is still, is still exactly
wrong.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.280
Greg Glassman is the founder of Crossfit,

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.580
which is a corporate donor to the nonprofit
that publishes Reason.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.280
Crossfit has disrupted the personal fitness
industry

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.820
by taking a radically different approach to
both nutrition and exercise.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.660
In contrast to much of the competition

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:59.320
and against the recommendations of many establishment scientists,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:04.280
Crossfit has always emphasized a high-intensity
workout and a low carb diet.

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:07.049
The approach has been wildly successful for
Crossfit,

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:13.700
which went from 13 gyms in 2005 to more than
13,000 across the world today.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:19.620
But Glassman says the rise to the top has
entailed near-constant battle with the opposition.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:24.569
And so we've made some nice enemies out of
a good chunk of the food and beverage industry

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:27.920
And all of its clowns that do its bidding.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.680
Crossfit’s explosive growth was made possible
in no small part

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.540
by the lack of regulation in the fitness industry.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:39.220
While many states require licenses for occupations
as innocuous as trimming trees,

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.720
tending bar, braiding hair, or even arranging
flowers,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.220
personal trainers can work without government
oversight.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:49.720
This arrangement allows Crossfit to have its
own certification program

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.829
that ignores most of the conventional nutrition
and exercise advice

00:02:53.829 --> 00:02:56.920
emanating from government and academic institutions.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.300
But over the past several years, competing
credentialing organizations have joined together

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:06.020
to lobby for government licensure of fitness
trainers in several states.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:11.320
That’s an attempt on silence Crossfit on
the subject of nutrition and exercise.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:16.560
The fight is occuring largely behind-the-scenes
at state legislatures across the country,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.380
where licensing laws have been introduced
on twenty-six separate occasions since 2005.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.620
But Crossfit supporters have pushed back just
as hard,

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.080
at times showing up in person to speak out
against the bills.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:32.859
The one place where Crossfit lost the battle
was Washington, DC,

00:03:32.859 --> 00:03:37.540
which passed the nation’s first fitness
trainer licensure law in 2014.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.150
Though even that hasn't gone fully into effect.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.020
If you control who gets to practice a profession

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.600
you also get to control how it's practiced.

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:48.620
Russ Greene is Crossfit’s point man in the
fight against government licensure.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:52.360
He says that licensing could kill a program
like Crossfit.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:57.400
Are we going to be free to talk to each other
about fitness and food

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:04.000
or is what we can say about them going to
be determined by federal and state policy?

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.640
State licensure is a protection racket and
that's all it is.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.879
Mark Rippetoe is a weightlifting coach and
author of the book Starting Strength,

00:04:13.879 --> 00:04:18.380
a program distinct from Crossfit but which
shares certain commonalities

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.590
like promoting training with barbells and
encouraging movements

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:25.640
that aren’t approved by establishment players
in the fitness industry.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:32.280
The state legislature that would adopt a state-wide
licensure program for exercise

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:38.160
is composed of people who do not understand
anything about the squat.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.180
Rippetoe and Crossfit trainers both promote
what they call a “full squat,”

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.760
which involves dipping below parallel, in
contrast to the less dramatic squat

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:50.120
promoted by the American College of Sports
Medicine.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:54.580
So what do you think they will say about our
below-parallel squat?

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:57.180
It'll be in the document as a standard of
practice

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:00.960
that squats cannot be done below parallel,
I promise you that.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.880
You're removing the ability of the purchaser
of the product

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.340
to determine what he wants to get done in
his strength and conditioning program

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:17.300
by telling him, essentially, that he must
buy the services of a licensed guy

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:23.360
who cannot prescribe below-parallel squats
because it's illegal now.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:30.360
The interaction or intersection of policy
and politics is a very problematic one.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:35.360
Holden McRae, a sports medicine professor
at Pepperdine University

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.040
and a member of a Crossfit gym,

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.360
says that the government’s record on fitness
guidance is spotty at best.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:47.380
Establishment fitness began when Frank Shorter
won the gold medal in the Olympic marathon

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:50.980
in Munich in 1972 and the running boom took
off.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:55.480
And then a big shift occurred in the mid 1990s.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.180
In 1995, the Surgeon General issued a report

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:01.289
that shifted the recommendations away from
vigorous activity

00:06:01.289 --> 00:06:03.880
towards low-to-moderate intensity

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:08.919
and de-emphasized certain fitness markers
like strength, agility, speed, power and coordination

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:12.200
putting most of the focus on cardiorespiratory
fitness.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:14.919
The guidelines were adopted by the American
Heart Association,

00:06:14.919 --> 00:06:19.349
the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention,
and the American College of Sports Medicine.

00:06:19.349 --> 00:06:22.940
The federal goverment’s National Activity
Guidelines, published by Congress,

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:26.880
recommend 150 minutes of moderate physical
activity a week.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.660
Moderate physical activity,

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:34.060
that requirement probably satisfied by walking
30 minutes five times a week.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:39.020
Most people do that accidentally, it's a very
low standard to hold a human being to.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.599
Doing yard work or walking slowly around the
block

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.440
that's not going to get you there, I'm sorry,
you actually have to work hard.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:48.260
The risk reduction for vigorous activity is
actually two times greater

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.030
compared to moderate exercise.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:56.099
It was an experiment that was run on the US
population with very little evidence to support

00:06:56.099 --> 00:06:57.099
it.

00:06:57.099 --> 00:06:59.180
Similar to what we had with the dietary guidelines

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:03.600
and I don't think there's enough transparency
with the conflicts of interest

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:06.540
you know, the involvement of big soda for
example.

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:09.360
Recent work out of the University of California
San Francisco

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:12.890
has uncovered evidence that the sugar lobby
paid off researchers

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:16.250
to downplay the link between sugar and heart
disease,

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:19.479
with the very first paper that placed the
blame on saturated fat

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:21.780
having clear ties to the sugar industry.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.500
There’s some evidence that the same special
interests

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:29.490
that used the government and universities

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.710
to influence nutrition science for the past
several decades

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:35.569
is employing similar tactics in the fitness
industry

00:07:35.569 --> 00:07:39.020
to promote the message it’s lack of physical
activity,

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:42.200
rather than sugar consumption, driving obesity.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:46.460
Most of the focus in the popular media and
in the scientific press

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:49.440
is, you know, they're eating too much, eating
too much, eating too much,

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:53.759
blaming fast food, blaming sugary drinks and
so on.

00:07:53.759 --> 00:07:57.199
And there's really virtually no compelling
evidence

00:07:57.199 --> 00:07:58.650
that that in fact is the cause.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:01.659
That’s from a video from the Global Energy
Balance Network,

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:06.000
a group forced to disband after its ties to
Coca Cola were revealed.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.180
At least one major group involved in the occupational
licensing fight

00:08:09.189 --> 00:08:11.479
has received support from soda companies.

00:08:11.479 --> 00:08:14.030
Coca Cola did not reply to requests to comment

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:18.010
and the American College of Sports Medicine
declined to participate in this story.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:23.260
But ASCM’s website does say that it only
advocates licensure for trainers working with clients

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:27.220
with “medical conditions that require minimal
to advanced clinical support.”

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:30.020
ASCM’s newest venture, Exercise is medicine,

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:32.070
is an attempt to create a credentialing system

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:35.320
to have doctors “prescribe” fitness trainers
to patients,

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:38.040
and it was underwritten by the Coca Cola company.

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:42.600
Even health organizations as powerful and
influential as the Centers for Disease Control

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.540
have recieved grants from Coca Cola.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:47.920
So Coca Cola is partnered with the Centers
for Disease Control and Prevention.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:53.660
That fact alone is sufficient to explain the
degree of the chronic disease crisis

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:55.160
that we're facing in this country today.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:09:00.580
These are 501C3 proxies that are able to keep
their doors open by the contributions from soda pop.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:03.440
When you think ACSM you better be thinking
Coca Cola

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:05.660
'cause that's what you got.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:10.320
The fields of nutrition and fitness are littered
with bad or incomplete science

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.840
that may take years or even decades more to
sort out.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:17.410
And this is why Rippetoe and Glassman are
skeptical

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:21.240
that government can do anything but make the
problem worse.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:26.500
The competative marketplace and people who
consume strength and conditioning instruction

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:28.640
is capable of sorting this out.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:31.240
Am I happy with my trainer?

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.080
Did I get stronger?

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:34.620
Did I get more fit?

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:41.060
These should be the criteria that a competative
marketplace provides for the profession.

